Z||000000||Madrox86GT||10-20-2000||12:12 AM||||ok i have a problem...when the car isnt in motion the oil pressure droms near zero....not good.  cause i will have to take it to someone to get the oil pump replaced.<P>2)i have a problem with my car when i press the gas good it starts to spudder...im dumbfounded on that one....<P>3) i have a small severed rubber hose and i cant seen to figure out where it goes and what its doing.<P>4) check engine light comes on and stays on constantly but i cant seem to find a problem of why it would come on...it did it ever since i got it in '97.<P>5)i have a squeeky alt. belt that when ever i get it replaced it keeps squeeking...i tried belt dressing but it stops it for a second but when i stop it does it again...but when i start it squeels but i throw it into neutral it stops and putting it back into gear stops it.<P><BR>if you all have some way of helping me, please by all means help me.<P>------------------<BR>You know my saying is:<P>-Red 84 SE for parts(or 300 all together)-<BR>-Trade/Sell 86 GT-<BR>-New Pioneer Raido and 4X10's-<P>-Silver V6 SE(wrecked) $300<BR>-Black 85 coupe(parts car)$500<BR>-or both black and sliver for $750<P>[This message has been edited by Madrox86GT (edited 10-20-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Madrox86GT (edited 10-20-2000).]||||reg||9
Z||000001||mrfiero||10-20-2000||01:03 AM||||1.  It could be a faulty gauge or oil pressure sending unit......have you checked either of these?  If your oil pump is bad and they have to drop the pan to change it out I would throw on some new rod bearings.....it couldn't hurt.  <P>2.  Bad/faulty TPS, a clogged catalytic convertor, fouled plugs, bad distributor cap/rotor, bad plug wires, clogged fuel filter.....it could be any of these or all of these or none of these! <P><BR>3.   Is it attached to the firewall right next to the valve cover?  If it is then it is no longer needed as it was removed in the recall.  It should have been capped off, but most dealers who did the recall work did a sloppy job.<P>4.   You need to check the codes.  Remove the black cigarette lighter panel and ground out the A & B terminals (the 2 next to each other on the passenger side of the outlet).  You can use a large paperclip to ground them to each other.  Now turn the ignition to "ON" and watch for the service light to flash.  It'll flash once, then pause then flash twice.....it'll do this 3 times.  This is the all is OK code and the stored codes will follow in the same fashion.  Write them down so you'll remember them....when it flashes 12 again you have retrieved all of the stored codes.  Disconnect the battery for 30-60 seconds to clear the codes.  Look in a Chilton/Haynes manual for what the codes mean (or post them here).<P>5.  Maybe it's not the belt.....it's possible that the bearings in the alternator itself are bad.  A friend of mine had a new alternator put on, but he overtightened the belt.  It burned out half the bearings and the alternator was shot in less than a year.  Remove the belt and try spinning the pulley by hand.....do you feel any resistance at any point or does it make a scraping noise?  If so, then your bearings are cooked (or are on their way to being cooked).    <P><BR>Hopefully this is of some use to you......good luck and let us know how you make out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||862M4inCA||10-20-2000||05:22 AM||||As for the sputtering, I'd first check for codes stored in the computer...might be something as simple as replacing the TPS, also, a better way to reset the computer without loosing all of your preset stations on the radio is to undo the quick disconnect terminal (similar looking to the one for the O2 sensor) that is located near the battery. This is the main power connection for the ECM. Have you tried connecting another gauge to determine if possibly your oil gauge/sender might be bad? Would maybe be a good idea to get a mechanical gauge and remove the sender unit and hook that one up to make sure its all reading correct. If it's still low, definately look into getting the pump replaced. Not good to run these things with low oil pressure. Also, someone correct me if I'm wrong, but after looking over this post, didn't Pontiac design some kinda safty feature into the sending unit to shut the fuel pump off when a low pressure condition occured? I've disconnected my sender but noticed that instead of dropping to zero, it pegged at the high side, indicating maximum pressure. As for your belt...first of all, I've found that if they are installed so that looking from the front of the engine you can read the numbers on the belt correctly (not upside down) that it tends not to squeal. Also remember a stupid mistake I made once resulting in the thing squealing and throwing the belt when I put in on the inside groove of the harmonic balancer (on the 4-cyl w/no AC) and not the outer groove. Might make sure it's installed on the correct groove. Hope this info helps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||10-20-2000||09:50 AM||||as for #1, is your preasure low, like 30-40 while driving, then down to below 10 while idling or parked? if so, get a mechanical guage and check it with that first, but that's the symptoms of a spun bearing. between the age of these cars and the poor oiling characteristics, it is very likely.<P>as for the vacuum line, I think that may be the same one I found last night. (runs along firewall, towards the passanger side of the engine bay, then was just hanging down) I looked arround and it was just long enough to reach the front side of the EGR vacuum thing. I put it on there and my idle smoothed out. But if the thing I just pluged it into is the EGR blow off valve, I'll plug the hose up. anyone know if that sounds like the right hose?||||reg||1
Z||000004||My7Fieros||10-20-2000||10:16 AM||||I wouldnt worry too much about the oil...every one of mine did that. One of them would even drop damn near to 0 at stop lights (red light would come on to)...I switched to a heavier wieght oil and havnt had a single problem since....<P>As far as the check engine light, pull the codes..<P>squeeky alt belt is usually something you have to deal with when owning a Fiero.||||reg||1
